"The Prism of a 2–Connected, Planar, Cubic Graph is Hamiltonian (A Proof Independent of the Four Colour Theorem) - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167506008704576)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Using the Four Colour Theorem, Rosenfeld and Barnette showed that the prism over any simple 3–polytope (which is a simple 4–polytope) is hamiltonian, [11].\n\nDate: 1988\n\nPages: 141-170\n\nVolume: Volume 41\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# The Prism of a 2–Connected, Planar, Cubic Graph is Hamiltonian (A Proof Independent of the Four Colour Theorem)\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel H. Fleischner\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/S0167-5060(08)70457-6\n\n\nIt is an open question whether the graphs of simple d-polytopes contain hamiltonian cycles for arbitrary d≥ 4. Using the Four Colour Theorem, Rosenfeld and Barnette showed that the prism over any simple 3–polytope (which is a simple 4–polytope) is hamiltonian, [11]. Later, Rosenfeld and Goodey were able to give a proof avoiding the use of the Four Colour Theorem provided the 3–polytope is cyclically 4–line-connected, [8]. Here we present a proof that the prism over any planar, 2–connected, 3–regular graph is hamiltonian, without using the Four Colour Theorem. This establishes a nontrivial necessary condition for the validity of the Four Colour Theorem.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHamilton cycles in prisms | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/227516586_Hamilton_cycles_in_prisms)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.9 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Hence, it is natural to ask if all 3-connected planar graphs can reach the level of \"Hamilton cycle in prism\" in the hamiltonian property hierarchy. ... The graph G is prism-hamiltonian if the prism over G has a Hamilton cycle.\n\n# Hamilton cycles in prisms\n\nJournal of Graph Theory\n\n  * December 2007\n  * 56(4):249 - 269\n\nDOI:10.1002/jgt.20250\n\n  * Source\n\nAuthors:\n\nTomáš Kaiser\n\nTomáš Kaiser\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nZdeněk Ryjáček\n\nZdeněk Ryjáček\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nDaniel Král\n\nDaniel Král\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nMoshe Rosenfeld\n\n  * University of Washington Tacoma\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe prism over a graph G is the Cartesian product G □ K2 of G with the complete graph K2. If G is hamiltonian, then G□K2 is also hamiltonian but the converse does not hold in general. Having a hamiltonian prism is shown to be an interesting relaxation of being hamiltonian. In this article, we examine classical problems on hamiltonicity of graphs in the context of having a hamiltonian prism. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJoseph Malkevitch: Hamiltonian circuits, Graph Codes, Error Correction Codes (https://web.york.cuny.edu/~malk/mycourses/gradcenter/ham.html)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.7 years ago; Does every 4-valent 4-polytope have a hamiltonian circuit? ... A graph H may not have a hamiltonian path (for example, K_{4,2}) but a prism formed from two copies of this graph (Figure 1) will have a hamiltonian circuit.\nFour-connected planar graphs are known to be hamiltonian by a theorem of Tutte. The major difficulty with d-connected graphs (d large, or even 4) is that it is not easy to determine if the graph is that of a d-polytope or not.\nAnother interesting class of questions arises from finding hamiltonian circuits on prisms formed from graphs. Given two copies of the same graph one can form a prism from them by joining corresponding vertices of the two graphs with edges. A graph H may not have a hamiltonian path (for example, K_{4,2}) but a prism formed from two copies of this graph (Figure 1) will have a hamiltonian circuit. These problems are related to questions about spanning trees of the polytopes. For 3-polytopes David Barnette showed that every such polytope has a spanning tree of maximal valence 3. However, for 4-polytopes there is no uniform value of k which will guarantee that a 4-polytope has a spanning tree of maximal valence k, a dramatic difference between the behavior of 3-polytopes and 4-polytopes.\n\nImage\nFigure 1\n\nReference:\nWilf, H. Combinatorial Algorithms: An Update, SIAM, Philadelphia, 1989.\nRyjáãek, Z. and T. Kaiser, D. Kráº, M. Rosenfeld, H.-J., Hamiltonian cycles in prisms over graphs, Journal of Graph Theory (to appear).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCoRe 2019 - Open Problems (https://hoanganhduc.github.io/events/CoRe2019/CoRe_2019_Open_Problems.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; (planar) cubic graphs can we guarantee to have (exponentially) many stable-tree ... What can we say about the structure of the reconﬁguration ... We are interested in the problem Maximal Induced Tree Reconfigura-\nthen a stable-tree decomposition exists. The question is the following: for which\n(planar) cubic graphs can we guarantee to have (exponentially) many stable-tree\ndecompositions? What can we say about the structure of the reconﬁguration\ngraph where a vertex is a stable-tree decomposition and two vertices (B1, W1)\nand (B2, W2) are adjacent iﬀW2 is obtained from W1 by removing a vertex v\nand adding the only vertex that lies in the intersection of the three paths joining\nthe three black neighbors of v in G[B1] ?\nUpdate:\nif G is cubic and planar, then it has at least n3 stable-tree decompo-\nsitions. Using the planarity of G, it might be possible to increase this exponent.\nKunihiro Wasa\nWe are interested in the problem Maximal Induced Tree Reconfigura-\ntion.\nThe input of the problem is two maximal induced trees S and T of\nG, and we want to know if there exists a reconﬁguration sequence between S\nand T, by Token Sliding or Token Jumping. The question is to determine the\ncomplexity of the problem.\nUpdate:\nThe problem has been solved by Haruka Mizuta. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Complexity of Induced Tree Reconfiguration Problems (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/transinf/E102.D/3/E102.D_2018FCP0010/_article)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.6 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Department of Systems Innovation Engineering, Faculty of Science and Engineering, Iwate University ... In this paper, we address the reconfiguration problem for induced trees, where an induced tree is a connected and acyclic induced subgraph of an input graph.\nIEICE Transactions on Information and Systems\n\nOnline ISSN : 1745-1361\nPrint ISSN : 0916-8532\n\n\nSpecial Section on Foundations of Computer Science — Algorithm, Theory of Computation, and their Applications —\n\nThe Complexity of Induced Tree Reconfiguration Problems\n\nKunihiro WASA, Katsuhisa YAMANAKA, Hiroki ARIMURA\n\nAuthor information\n\n  * Kunihiro WASA\n\nNational Institute of Informatics\n\n  * Katsuhisa YAMANAKA\n\nDepartment of Systems Innovation Engineering, Faculty of Science and Engineering, Iwate University\n\n  * Hiroki ARIMURA\n\nGraduate School of Information Science and Technology, Hokkaido University\n\n#### Corresponding author\n\nImage: ORCID\n\nKeywords: reconfiguration problem, induced trees, PSPACE-complete, W[1]-hard, FPT\n\nJOURNAL FREE ACCESS\n\n2019 Volume E102.D Issue 3 Pages 464-469\n\nDOI https://doi.org/10.1587/transinf.2018FCP0010\n\nDetails\n\n  * Published: March 01, 2019 Manuscript Received: March 30, 2018 Released on J-STAGE: March 01, 2019 Accepted: - Advance online publication: - Manuscript Revised: July 27, 2018\n\nDownload PDF (404K)\n\nText\n\nHow to download citation\n\nContact us\n\nArticle overview\n\n  * Abstract\n  * References (20)\n  * Content from these authors\n\nAbstract\n\nGiven two feasible solutions A and B, a reconfiguration problem asks whether there exists a reconfiguration sequence (A_{0}=A, A_{1},...,A_{ℓ}=B) such that (i) A_{0},...,A_{ℓ} are feasible solutions and (ii) we can obtain A_{i} from A_{i-1} under the prescribed rule (the reconfiguration rule) for each i ∈ {1,...,ℓ}. In this paper, we address the reconfiguration problem for induced trees, where an induced tree is a connected and acyclic induced subgraph of an input graph. We consider the following two rules as the prescribed rules: Token Jumping: removing u from an induced tree and adding v to the tree, and Token Sliding: removing u from an induced tree and adding v adjacent to u to the tree, where u and v are vertices of an input graph. As the main results, we show that (I) the reconfiguration problemis PSPACE-complete even if the input graph is of bounded maximum degree, (II) the reconfiguration problem is W[1]-hard when parameterized by both the size of induced trees and the length of the reconfiguration sequence, and (III) there exists an FPT algorithm when the problem is parameterized by both the size of induced trees and the maximum degree of an input graph under Token Jumping and Token Sliding.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) A characterization of Hamiltonian prisms (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/229540767_A_characterization_of_Hamiltonian_prisms)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; A characterization is established for a graph G to have a Hamilton cycle in G × K2, the prism over G. ... It is also shown that if P is a simple 3-polytope all of whose faces are polygons with six sides or less than the prism over P admits a Hamiltonian circuit.\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# A characterization of Hamiltonian prisms\n\n  * June 1993\n  * Journal of Graph Theory 17(2):161 - 171\n\nDOI:10.1002/jgt.3190170205\n\nAuthors:\n\nP. Paulraja\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nA characterization is established for a graph G to have a Hamilton cycle in G × K2, the prism over G. Moreover, it is shown that every 3-connected graph has a 2-connected spanning bipartite subgraph. Using this result, the existence of a Hamilton cycle in the prism over every 3-connected cubic graph is established. Further, the existence of a Hamilton cycle in the prism over a cubic 2-connected graph is also discussed. Earlier results in this direction are shown to be particular cases of the results obtained here. © 1993 John Wiley & Sons, Inc.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nContent uploaded by P. Paulraja\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area was uploaded by P. Paulraja on Jan 31, 2021\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nA\n\nCharacterization\n\nof\n\nHamiltonian Prisms\n\nP.\n\nPaulraja\n\nDEPARTMENT\n\nOF\n\nMATHEMATICS\n\nANNAMALAI UNIVERSITY\n\nANNA MA LAIN AGAR, INDIA\n\nABSTRACT\n\nA\n\ncharacterization is established for\n\na\n\ngraph\n\nG\n\nto have\n\na\n\nHamilton\n\ncycle in\n\nG\n\nx\n\nK2,\n\nthe prism over\n\nG.\n\nMoreover, it is shown that every\n\n3-connected graph has\n\na\n\n2-connected spanning bipartite subgraph.\n\nUsing this result, the existence of\n\na\n\nHamilton cycle in the prism over\n\nevery 3-connected cubic graph is established. Further, the existence\n\nof\n\na\n\nHamilton cycle in the prism over\n\na\n\ncubic 2-connected graph is also\n\ndiscussed. Earlier results in this direction are shown to be particular\n\ncases of the results obtained here.\n\n0\n\n1993\n\nJohn\n\nWiley\n\n&\n\nSons,\n\nInc.\n\n1.\n\nINTRODUCTION AND PRELIMINARIES\n\nThe Cartesian product of two graphs\n\nG\n\nand\n\nH\n\nis defined as follows:\n\nV(G\n\nX\n\nH)\n\n=\n\nV(G)\n\nX\n\nV(H),\n\nand\n\nE(G\n\nX\n\nH)\n\n=\n\n{(gl,hl)(g2,h2):\n\neither\n\ngl\n\n=\n\ng2\n\nand\n\nhlh2\n\nE\n\nE(H)\n\nor else\n\nglg2\n\nE\n\nE(G)\n\nand\n\nhl\n\n=\n\nh2}.\n\nG\n\nX\n\nK2\n\nis viewed as a graph obtained from\n\nG\n\nby taking two disjoint copies\n\nof\n\nG,\n\nsay\n\nG1\n\nand\n\nG2,\n\nand joining their “corresponding” vertices.\n\nG\n\nX\n\nK2\n\nis called\n\nthe\n\nprism\n\nover\n\nG.\n\nAn\n\nedge of the prism over\n\nG,\n\njoining one copy of\n\nG\n\nto\n\nthe other is called a\n\npillar\n\nof the prism. In this paper, we are interested in\n\nfinding a Hamilton cycle in the prism over a graph.\n\nA\n\ngraph is said to be cyclically n-edge-connected if it cannot be discon-\n\nnected into components, two of which contain a cycle, by deleting fewer\n\nthan n edges.\n\nAll\n\nthe definitions that are not given here may be found in\n\nThe problem under consideration has its origin in the conjecture of\n\nBarnette\n\n[6,\n\np. 11451 that the graph of each simple 4-polytope\n\n[6]\n\nhas a\n\nHamilton cycle. Assuming the four-color theorem, Rosenfeld and Barnette\n\n[9] proved the existence of Hamilton cycles in 4-dimensional prisms having\n\ncubic 3-connected planar graphs as “bases.” In\n\n[5]\n\nGoodey and Rosenfeld\n\nproved that if\n\nG\n\nis\n\na simple cyclically 4-edge-connected cubic plane graph\n\nthen\n\nG\n\nX\n\nK2\n\nadmits a Hamilton cycle. Later, Fleischner [4] proved that if\n\nJournal\n\nof\n\nGraph\n\nTheory,\n\nVol.\n\n17,\n\nNo.\n\n2,\n\n161 -171 (1993)\n\nPI,\n\n~61, and 171.\n\n0\n\n1993\n\nJohn\n\nWiley\n\n&\n\nSons, Inc.\n\nCCC\n\n0364-9024/93/020161-1\n\nI\n\n162\n\nJOURNAL\n\nOF\n\nGRAPH THEORY\n\nG\n\nis a cubic 2-connected planar graph, then the prism over\n\nG\n\nis hamiltonian.\n\nRecently, Alspach and Rosenfeld\n\n[l]\n\nstudied the existence of a pair of\n\nedge-disjoint Hamilton cycles in the prisms over 3-connected cubic graphs.\n\nIn this paper, we consider the existence of a Hamilton cycle in the prism\n\nover a graph\n\nG,\n\nwhere\n\nG\n\nis not necessarily planar. Actually, we characterize\n\nthose graphs for which the prism is hamiltonian. Finding a Hamilton cycle\n\nin the prism over\n\nG\n\nis proved to be an NP-complete problem\n\n[4].\n\nBefore presenting the results and their consequences, we state a result of\n\nFleischner\n\n[3]\n\nfor our future reference.\n\nTheorem\n\nA.\n\ndistinct vertices\n\nof\n\nG.\n\nThen there exist subgraphs\n\nE\n\nand\n\nP\n\nof\n\nG\n\nsuch that\n\nLet\n\nG\n\nbe a 2-edge-connected graph, and let\n\nv\n\nand\n\nw,\n\nbe\n\n1.\n\nS\n\n=\n\nE\n\nU\n\nP\n\nis a connected spanning subgraph of\n\nG,\n\n2.\n\nE\n\nis an edge-disjoint union of cycles,\n\n3.\n\nP\n\nis a path-forest,\n\n4.\n\nE\n\nand\n\nP\n\nare edge-disjoint, and\n\n5.\n\nu'E\n\nV(E)\n\n-\n\nV(P) and\n\nw\n\nE\n\nV(E)\n\n-\n\nZ(P), where Z(P) denotes the\n\ninterior vertices of P.\n\nA subgraph\n\nH\n\nof a connected graph\n\nG\n\nis called an EP-subgraph of\n\nG\n\nif\n\nthe following conditions are satisfied:\n\n(a)\n\nH\n\nis a connected spanning subgraph of\n\nG,\n\n(b)\n\nA(H)\n\n5\n\n4,\n\nand\n\n(c)\n\nH\n\n=\n\nE\n\nU\n\nP,\n\nwhere\n\nE\n\nis an edge-disjoint union of cycles,\n\nP\n\nis a\n\nvertex-disjoint union of paths, such that no vertex of a path of\n\nP\n\nis\n\nof degree\n\n4\n\nin\n\nH,\n\nand\n\nE\n\nand\n\nP\n\nare edge-disjoint.\n\nIt is easy to observe that in an EP-subgraph\n\nH\n\nof\n\nG,\n\nif we duplicate the\n\nedges of the paths\n\nof\n\nP, we get an eulerian multigraph\n\nH'\n\nof maximum\n\ndegree\n\n4.\n\nIn the sequel,\n\nH'\n\nalways represents the eulerian multigraph that\n\narises out of an EP-subgraph\n\nH\n\nof a graph\n\nG.\n\nIf\n\nH'\n\nadmits an even cycle\n\ndecomposition, then we call the EP-subgraph\n\n\nBook\n\n  * Jan 1976\n\n  * J.A. Bondy\n  * U.S.R. Murty\n\nView\n\nParity of Cycles Containing Specified Edges\n\nArticle\n\n  * Dec 1985\n\n  * W.D. McCuaig\n  * Moshe Rosenfeld\n\nCycles through specified elements in k-connected graphs are a popular topic of investigation. The chapter overviews that in a cubic 2-connected planar graph, any edge that does not belong to a two-edge cut is a chord of an even cycle. It highlights that this result holds in much more general situations (that is, planarity is not needed). The chapter proves that in 3-connected graphs, every pair of edges is almost always contained both in an even and an odd cycle. A full characterization of the exceptional cases is also obtained. Similar results for regular graphs are obtained in the chapter.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nGraph theory with applications (revised edition), by J. A. Bondy and U.S.R. Murty. Pp x, 264. £5·95 paperback. 1977. SBN 0 333 22694 1 (Macmillan)\n\nArticle\n\n  * Sep 2016\n  * Math Gaz\n\n  * E. Keith Lloyd\n\nView\n\nHamiltonian circuits in prisms over certain simple 3-polytopes\n\nArticle\n\n  * Dec 1978\n  * DISCRETE MATH\n\n  * P. R. Goodey\n  * Moshe Rosenfeld\n\nIn this paper it is shown that the prisms over cyclically 4-connect simple 3-polytopes admit Hamiltonian circuits. It is also shown that if P is a simple 3-polytope all of whose faces are polygons with six sides or less than the prism over P admits a Hamiltonian circuit.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nHamiltonian circuits in certain prisms\n\nArticle\n\n  * Aug 1973\n  * DISCRETE MATH\n\n  * Moshe Rosenfeld\n  * David W. Barnette\n\nView\n\nPolytopes, graphs, and complexes\n\nArticle\n\n  * Nov 1970\n  * B AM MATH SOC\n\n  * Branko Grünbaum\n\nView\n\nOn Spanning Subgraphs of a Connected Bridgeless Graph and Their Application to DT1-Graphs\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n464\nIEICE TRANS. INF. & SYST., VOL.E102–D, NO.3 MA (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/transinf/E102.D/3/E102.D_2018FCP0010/_pdf)\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; The Complexity of Induced Tree Reconﬁguration Problems ... reconﬁguration problem, induced trees, PSPACE-complete, ... gineering, Faculty of Science and Engineering, Iwate University,\n464\nIEICE TRA